{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LIVE EVIDENCE UPDATE — two of the three unmet criteria now HAVE a passing attestation; the third is\nblocked by a test defect, not by this story's code. Recording precisely which is which rather than\nclaiming the set.\n\nSOURCE: GitHub Actions run 30652534806, job \"jira DC harness smoke (real Jira Data Center,\nephemeral)\", on main @ f1a14e0c5 — which contains this story's merged change (4e4aadda1). This\nSUPERSEDES run 30637886718, the only run previously attested here, which predates both the oracle\ncorrection and the PAT-cap fix that let the suite reach these tests at all.\n\nCRITERION: \"an immediately repeated writing pass writes nothing (idempotence) —\n`test_a_repeated_dc_reconcile_pass_writes_nothing` passes\" — **MET.** That test does NOT appear in the\nrun's FAILED list; the only two failures are named below. It previously failed because it asserted\n`_envelope()` on a writing-mode pass, which emits no JSON (`__main__.py:444-452`) — an assertion that\ncould never have passed. That correction is what makes this run meaningful.\n\nCRITERION: \"a full writing `bootstrap-strict` pass converges with no unhandled exception\" — **MET for\nthe repeated-pass test**, which runs `bootstrap-strict` and calls `_assert_no_unhandled_exception`.\nPartially met overall, because the second covering test is the one below.\n\nCRITERION: \"a DC-created ticket carries `creation_channel == 'jira'` —\n`test_a_dc_created_ticket_carries_the_shared_jira_provenance` passes\" — **NOT MET, and it CANNOT be met\nuntil [rebar:23ed-de0a-765b-49aa] is fixed.** That test fails on an assertion placed BEFORE the\ncreation_channel check:\n\n    assert matched, f\"no local ticket was created from DC issue {remote_key}\"\n\n`matched` is computed as `remote_key in json.dumps(t)` using the RAW Jira key. It cannot ever be true:\n`_jira_key_to_local_id` lowercases (`inbound_translate.py:120-124`, `RBJVTVC-1` -> `jira-rbjvtvc-1`),\nand the inbound CREATE payload (`apply_inbound_records.py:183-212`) carries no raw Jira key — it\nappears only as a TITLE FALLBACK, unused whenever the issue has a summary, as it does here. Verified\nagainst the exact payload shape: `remote_key in json.dumps(ticket)` is False.\n\nSo the `creation_channel` assertion this criterion names is never reached. The failure is an ORACLE\ndefect, not evidence about this story's code — and, symmetrically, a green from it would prove nothing\neither until the assertion targets something the inbound path actually writes. I am NOT asking for\nthis criterion to be waived: it should stay unmet until 23ed lands and the harness job shows the test\npassing on its own terms.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT CLAIMING. I am not claiming the inbound create works. The run reports\n`invariants: scanned=1` and `OK: applied 1 of 1 mutations`, which is CONSISTENT with a correct\ninbound create, but consistency is not proof and this oracle cannot supply it. Epic e369's headline\nacceptance criterion remains UNPROVEN — neither demonstrated nor refuted. That is the honest state,\nand it is why 23ed exists."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785521450132706001,"uuid":"da575ecd-aa17-44ee-90ef-3a311dd48309"}